            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wernher von Braun: Early Life</strong><br>\nWernher von Braun was born in 1912 in the small town of Wirsitz, in what was then the German Empire. From a young age, he was fascinated with the idea of space flight and astronomy, influenced by science fiction and the work of spaceflight pioneers like Hermann Oberth. By the age of 20, he was already involved in rocket experiments, laying the groundwork for his future career in rocket science and space exploration.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Nazi Era and V-2 Rockets</strong><br>\nDuring the Nazi era, von Braun's work took a significant turn as he became the technical director of the Peenemünde Army Research Center. Here, he developed the V-2 rocket, the world's first long-range guided ballistic missile. The V-2 rocket was a breakthrough in technology, but it was also used by the Nazi regime to bomb Allied cities during World War II, causing significant casualties.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War Challenges</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, von Braun faced the moral and ethical implications of his work for the Nazi regime. He surrendered to American forces and was secretly moved to the United States under Operation Paperclip, along with other scientists. This operation was controversial but allowed the U.S. to gain advanced technology and knowledge from German scientists.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
