      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contribution to American Space Effort</strong><br>\nIn the United States, von Braun became a key figure in the American space effort. He worked for the U.S. Army to develop ballistic missiles before joining NASA. His expertise was critical in designing the Saturn V rocket, which would eventually take humans to the Moon. He is often credited with being the preeminent figure in the birth of the U.S. space program.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Saturn V and the Moon Landing</strong><br>\nVon Braun's vision and technical acumen culminated in the development of the Saturn V rocket, the most powerful rocket ever built at the time. It was used for the Apollo Moon-landing missions, making von Braun a central figure in achieving President Kennedy's goal of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely to Earth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Educator and Visionary</strong><br>\nBeyond engineering, von Braun was also an educator and a visionary. He was a prolific writer and speaker, using his public platform to educate the public about space exploration. He advocated for space science in the media and inspired millions with his vision of future space exploration and human colonization of other planets.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
